<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Other Simulations</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">See Figures <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix A Other Simulations ‣ Tool Building as a Path to “Superintelligence”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A1.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix A Other Simulations ‣ Tool Building as a Path to “Superintelligence”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, as well as Table <a href="#A1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Appendix A Other Simulations ‣ Tool Building as a Path to “Superintelligence”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21061v1/plot2_heatmap_diagnostic.png" id="A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/176;" width="238" height="176" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A1.F8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">As both <math id="A1.F8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.F8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> increase, the probability of an estimator with imperfect information begins to collapse to zero. Only Estimator <math id="A1.F8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is able to consistently produce the next monomial. The above heatmap was constructed through generating <math id="A1.F8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> circuits for each combination of hyperparameters and computing the corresponding <math id="A1.F8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="A1.F8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. This diagram shows the performance when not using the adversarial dataset construction. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21061v1/plot_gamma_bar_abcd.svg" id="A1.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/175;" width="238" height="175"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A1.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> The figure above shows how <math id="A1.F9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> changes for Estimators <math id="A1.F9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.F9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.F9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> when the data is biased adversarially (as in the results so far) preventing identification monomials from frequency statistics, and without de-biasing.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="A1.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Likelihood-based fit of LLM accuracy to an effective-prefix model.<span id="A1.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Accuracy as a function of prefix length <math id="A1.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is fit using two one-parameter models for the effective number of known prefix terms: proportional <math id="A1.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=ug" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=ug</annotation></semantics></math> and constant-capacity <math id="A1.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=v</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fits use the binomial log-likelihood aggregated across depths; models are compared via AIC.
Reported <math id="A1.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathrm{AIC}=\mathrm{AIC}_{\text{constant}}-\mathrm{AIC}_{\text{proportional}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>AIC</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>AIC</mi><mtext>constant</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>AIC</mi><mtext>proportional</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathrm{AIC}=\mathrm{AIC}_{\text{constant}}-\mathrm{AIC}_{\text{proportional}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so positive values favor proportional scaling.
Values <math id="A1.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathrm{AIC}&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>AIC</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathrm{AIC}&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math> indicate no meaningful distinction.</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="A1.T1.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
